/* Scanned paper, embedded.
 *
 * Simulating this was the wrong instinct. Ageing is the accumulated history of
 * a physical object -- damp, handling, foxing, the fibre itself -- and the
 * cheapest honest way to have it is to photograph paper that already has it.
 * Two scans rather than one, at different scales, because a single tile repeats
 * visibly however good it is.
 *
 * Both are public domain, from Wikimedia Commons. See NOTICE.
 *
 *   paper A  Old paper6.jpg        -- even mottling and foxing, no fold, no vignette
 *   paper B  Old Paper texture.jpg -- centre crop: bare fibre, no landmark at all
 *
 * B is deliberately featureless. The first attempt used a scan folded in
 * quarters, and the fold survived the crop: rotated ninety degrees and tiled,
 * it drew a ladder of vertical bands straight down the sheet. An under-layer
 * has to be texture without incident, or the tiling announces itself.
 *
 * Both are cropped hard. A scan's own edges are where its artefacts live -- the
 * first crop of A kept a band of faint ribbing down one side, and mirroring
 * turned it into a ladder of marks straight down the sheet, once per tile.
 * Trimmed of their scan borders, downscaled and re-compressed: at the size a
 * tile occupies on the sheet these carry about 300 pixels per inch, and paper
 * grain has no sharp detail to lose in any case.
 */

export const PAPER_A = "data:image/jpeg;base64,";

export const PAPER_B = "data:image/jpeg;base64,";
